N('grace-episcopal-middletown','Grace Episcopal Church','Middletown','midh','worship','standing',41.44675,-74.41989,'exact','open','drive','','1846–1868',
  'The first Grace Church was built in 1846 and 1847 and first used on December 24, 1847. It was enlarged in the Gothic Revival style from 1866 to 1868, with transepts paid for by Elisha Wheeler, and its wooden steeple was taken down between 1868 and 1882 (the parish history). A Collegiate Gothic parish house followed in 1913. It was listed on the National Register in 2010.',
  'The Gothic Revival church with its off-set bell tower, stepped buttresses and pointed windows, at North and Depot streets. The parish runs the Guild of St. Margaret soup kitchen, open daily since 1980, and has a new access ramp. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Grace Episcopal Church, 58 North Street (office at 12 Depot Street); parking nearby (the parish page). Services from the parish home page, read November 21, 2026.',
  [W('Grace Episcopal Church (Middletown, New York)'),L('Grace Episcopal Church','https://gracechurchmiddletown.org/'),L('Grace Church history','https://gracechurchmiddletown.org/?p=176')],
  brick='?',hours='Sunday Mass 9:30 a.m.; Wednesday healing Mass 10 a.m.'),
N('first-congregational-middletown','First Congregational Church','Middletown','midh','worship','standing',41.44573,-74.41883,'exact','view','drive','','1872 (congregation 1785)',
  'New England settlers organized the congregation on June 10, 1785, and for forty years it was the only church in the area; its founding is said to mark the beginning of Middletown as a village. The present Gothic Revival church, the third, was built in 1872 to designs by Isaac G. Perry, and its spire is the tallest in central Middletown. It was listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'The 1872 Perry church and spire at 29 East Main Street, with the 1935 state marker at East Main and William streets. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Congregation\'s own page not found, so service times not checked: view from the street.',
  [W('First Congregational Church of Middletown'),HM('First Congregational Church',47897)],
  brick='?'),
N('hillside-cemetery-middletown','Hillside Cemetery','Middletown','midh','burial','standing',41.44293,-74.42961,'site','public','walk','cemeteries','1861',
  'A 52-acre rural cemetery on Mulberry Street opened in 1861, designed by Calvert Vaux and Frederick Law Olmsted. Several thousand people are buried there, among them three Civil War Medal of Honor men and Congressman Moses D. Stivers. It was listed on the National Register in 1994.',
  'Curving drives cut into a steep hillside, a pond near the top and the drained remains of Vaux\'s lower pond, 19th-century funerary art, and at the front gate a Gothic Revival stone office of 1930, disused since a garage replaced it in 1992 (Wikipedia).',
  '',
  'Working cemetery on Mulberry Street, its drives open to walkers; owner and hours not checked from a page of its own, so keep to the drives in daylight. Street parking on Mulberry Street (not checked).',
  [W('Hillside Cemetery (Middletown, New York)')],
  brick='?'),
N('primitive-baptist-church-brookfield','Primitive Baptist Church of Brookfield (Old School Baptist Meeting House)','Slate Hill, Town of Wawayanda','midh','worship','standing',41.39028,-74.47389,'site','view','drive','','1792',
  'Baptists began meeting at nearby Woodlawn Farm in 1783, incorporated in 1791, and built this meeting house in 1792 on land given by Joseph Hallock, when the hamlet was still called Brookfield. The last member died in 1933, but trustees kept the building and held an annual service with an Old School Baptist pastor from North Carolina; in 2004 two members were voted in and the congregation has grown again. It was listed on the National Register in 1976.',
  'A tall white clapboard meeting house on a rise north of US 6, with a later four-story tower and spire. Most of the original window sashes survive on the north and west sides; inside are box pews with raised and fielded panels, all in unpainted white pine, and a pulpit of a late 17th- and early 18th-century New England type (Wikipedia). Interior seen only at services; times not checked.',
  'A 1792 meeting house with its box pews, kept alive by an annual service through seventy years with no members.',
  'Congregation\'s own page not found, so service times not checked: view from US 6.',
  [W('Primitive Baptist Church of Brookfield')],
  brick='no'),
